//
// AN OPERATOR IS NOT COMMUTED ONTO THE RIGHT OPERAND'S TYPE (`@FR-Op-Left` crossed with
// `@FR-Disp-Select`).  `2 + a` asks the LEFT operand's type — `integer` — for a `plus` taking a `V`;
// the selection is among `integer`'s members only ("the right operand's type is never asked and
// no form is commuted"), so `V`'s `plus(self: V, o: integer)` does not answer it, and with no
// applicable member the expression is refused at compile time.
//
// @falsified-at: none — a conformance guard; refused as the rule says, interpret and native.
// CHANNEL: the compile (the expected diagnostic).
// ARMED: no instrument.
// WITNESS: the diagnostic names both operand types in source order.
// HOLDS: `an-operator-picks-the-left-types-member-by-the-right-operand-as-a-call-does` beside it
//   (`a + 2` answers).
// @EXPECT_ERROR: No matching operator '+' on 'integer' and 'V'

struct V { x: integer }

operator plus(self: V, o: integer) -> V { V { x: self.x + o * 10 } }

fn main() {
  a = V { x: 1 };
  r = 2 + a;
  println("{r.x}");
}
